  "content": "To turn a boat while sailing, you use the steering wheel and throttle together. The boat is steered by jet thrust: water is sucked in, pressurized by the impeller in the jet pump, and expelled through the thrust nozzles. Steering control depends on the combination of steering wheel position and engine speed (throttle). More throttle produces higher thrust, causing the boat to turn more sharply; less throttle results in lower thrust, making the turn more gradual. <PIC> It is important not to pull the remote control levers back to idle when trying to steer away from objects—you need throttle to steer. Also, ensure passengers are holding on before making turns to avoid falls.",
