big_answer.raw_response:
{
  "content": "To cross wakes and swells, adjust your speed and the angle at which you approach them. The best method is to “quarter” the wake—cross at about a 45-degree angle—which reduces the boat’s tendency to steer away from the wake and minimizes the jolt. Slower speeds generally help smooth the crossing. Be aware that crossing a group of wakes or swells is more challenging than a single one, and sharp wakes produce a stronger jolt than broad swells. Always be prepared to steer and balance as needed. <PIC>",
  "images": ["Manual09_249", "Manual09_250"]
}

