        "Connecting Regulator To The LP Tank"
      ],
      "section_title": "Connecting Regulator To The LP Tank",
      "text": "Nipple has to be centered into the LP tank valve.\n\n6. Turn the coupling nut clockwise and tighten to a full stop. The regulator will seal on the back-check feature in the LP tank valve, resulting in some resistance. An additional one-half to three-quarters turn is required to complete the connection. Tighten by hand only - do not use tools.\n\n**NOTE:**\n\nIf you cannot complete the connection, disconnect regulator and repeat steps 5 and 6. If you are still unable to complete the connection, do not use this regulator!\n\n![Illustration](插图/Manual19_19.jpg)",
      "images": [
        "Manual19_19"
      ]
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "doc_name": "Outdoor Grill",
      "header_path": [
        "Outdoor Grill Use and Care Guide",
        "Connecting Regulator To The LP Tank"
      ],
      "section_title": "Connecting Regulator To The LP Tank",
      "text": "- Do not insert any tool or foreign objects into the valve outlet or safety relief valve. You may damage the valve and cause a leak. Leaking propane may result in explosion, fire, severe personal injury, or death.\n\n- If a leak is detected at any time, STOP and call the fire department.\n\n- If you cannot stop a gas leak, immediately close LP tank valve and call LP gas supplier or your fire department!",
      "images": []
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "doc_name": "Outdoor Grill",
      "header_path": [
        "Outdoor Grill Use and Care Guide",
        "Leak Testing Valves, Hose and Regulator"
      ],
      "section_title": "Leak Testing Valves, Hose and Regulator",
      "text": "## Leak Testing Valves, Hose and Regulator\n\n1. Turn all grill control knobs to OFF.\n\n2. Be sure regulator is tightly connected to LP tank.\n\n3. Completely open LP tank valve by turning OPD hand wheel counterclockwise. If you hear a rushing sound, turn gas off immediately. There is a major leak at the connection. Correct before proceeding by calling Sears for replacement parts at 1-800-4-MY-HOME.\n\n4. Brush soapy solution on to areas where bubbles are shown in picture below:\n\n![Illustration](插图/Manual19_20.jpg)\n\n5. If \"growing\" bubbles appear, there is a leak. Close LP tank valve immediately and retighten connections. If leaks cannot be stopped, do not try to repair. Call Sears for replacement parts at 1-800-4-MY-HOME.\n\n6. Always close LP tank valve after performing leak test by turning hand wheel clockwise.",
      "images": [
        "Manual19_20"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

small_answer.raw_response:
{
  "content": "1. Turn all grill control knobs to OFF. 2. Be sure regulator is tightly connected to the LP tank. 3. Completely open the LP tank valve by turning the OPD hand wheel counterclockwise. If you hear a rushing sound, turn gas off immediately (major leak at connection; call Sears at 1-800-4-MY-HOME). 4. Mix a 50/50 mild soap and water solution. Using a clean paintbrush, brush the soapy solution onto the areas where bubbles are shown in the picture below. <PIC> 5. If \"growing\" bubbles appear, there is a leak. Close the LP tank valve immediately and retighten connections. If the leak cannot be stopped, do not try to repair; call Sears at 1-800-4-MY-HOME. 6. Always close the LP tank valve after performing the leak test by turning the hand wheel clockwise. Important safety: Perform the leak test outdoors in a well-ventilated area away from ignition sources. Do not smoke or use an open flame during the test. Repeat the leak test each time the LP tank is exchanged or refilled.",
  "images": ["Manual19_20"]
}

