  "content": "The two remote control levers (one for each engine) control both throttle and shifting. To move forward, push the levers forward from the neutral position; that first engages the forward gear, and further movement increases engine speed. To go in reverse, pull the levers backward from neutral; again, initial movement engages reverse, and more travel increases thrust. The levers are normally operated together for balanced cruising. The engines cannot start unless the levers are in the neutral position (start-in-gear protection). At low forward speeds, the shift gates are only partially lifted, directing thrust downward for better steering response (Thrust Directional Enhancer). The images below illustrate the shift gate positions as the levers are moved forward.",
